IV. Postman Collection and Environment Variables
To manage the comprehensive set of API tests detailed in this document effectively, leveraging Postman's organizational and automation features is highly recommended. This section provides guidance on structuring a Postman Collection, utilizing Environment Variables for dynamic data and configuration, and introducing basic scripting for test assertions and request chaining. These practices move beyond manual, ad-hoc testing towards a more robust, maintainable, and potentially automatable testing process.
A. Guidance on Structuring the Postman Collection
A Postman Collection serves as a container for organizing related API requests. For the Digital Biomarker Data Collection Service, a dedicated collection should be created.
•	Create a Collection:
o	In Postman, create a new collection (e.g., named "Digital Biomarker Service API Tests").
•	Organize with Folders:
o	Within this collection, create folders to group requests by resource or functionality. This mirrors the structure of Section II. Examples:
	"Root Endpoint"
	"Patient Management"
	"Experiment Management"
	"Patient Data Management"
	"Authentication and Authorization Tests"
•	Clear Request Naming:
o	Name each request descriptively to indicate its purpose and expected outcome. Examples:
	Inside "Patient Management" folder:
	"Create Patient - Valid Data"
	"Create Patient - Missing Name Field"
	"Get Patient by ID - Found"
	"Get Patient by ID - Not Found (Invalid ID)"
	"Update Patient - Change Age"
	"Delete Patient - Successful"
•	Add Descriptions:
o	Utilize the description field for folders and individual requests to provide context, outline prerequisites (e.g., "Requires a valid patient_id from 'Create Patient' request"), or note specific verification points.
A well-structured collection is significantly easier to navigate, understand, and maintain, especially as the number of test cases grows. This organization is invaluable for team collaboration and for onboarding new members to the testing process. It transforms a potentially chaotic list of requests into a managed and scalable test suite.
B. Utilizing Environment Variables
Postman Environment Variables allow for the abstraction of common or dynamic values from individual requests, making the test suite more flexible, portable, and easier to manage.
•	Create an Environment:
o	In Postman, create a new environment (e.g., "Local Development Environment").
•	Define Key Variables:
o	base_url: The base URL of the API service.
	Initial Value: http://127.0.0.1:5000
o	username: The username for Basic Authentication.
	Initial Value: user
o	password: The password for Basic Authentication.
	Initial Value: password (Mark as "secret" type in Postman for masking)
o	patient_id: This variable will be dynamically populated by scripts after a patient is created (see Section IV.C). Leave its initial value empty or set to a placeholder.
o	experiment_id: Dynamically populated after an experiment is created. Leave initial value empty.
o	data_point_id: Dynamically populated after a data point is added. Leave initial value empty.
•	Referencing Variables in Requests:
o	In your Postman requests, replace hardcoded values with environment variable placeholders using double curly braces:
	Request URL: {{base_url}}/patient/{{patient_id}}
	Authorization Username: {{username}}
	Authorization Password: {{password}}
	Request Body (e.g., for POST /patient/<patient_id>/data):
JSON
{
    "experimentId": "{{experiment_id}}",
    //... other fields
}
Using environment variables offers several advantages:
•	Portability: If the API is deployed to a different server (e.g., staging, production) with a new URL or credentials, only the environment variables need to be updated, not every individual request in the collection.
•	Maintainability: Centralizes configuration details, making them easier to find and modify.
•	Security: Passwords can be masked in the Postman UI when set as "secret" type variables.
•	Dynamic Data Flow: Crucially, environment variables are the standard Postman mechanism for passing data generated in one request (like a patient_id from a POST response) to subsequent requests that depend on that data. This is far more efficient and less error-prone than manually copying and pasting IDs.
C. Scripting in Postman (Tests Tab)
Postman allows JavaScript code to be executed in the "Pre-request Script" and "Tests" tabs of a request. "Tests" scripts are executed after the response is received and are primarily used for response validation and for capturing data to be used in subsequent requests (request chaining).
•	Writing Basic Assertions (Response Validation): Postman provides a pm.test() function and a ChaiJS BDD assertion library (pm.expect()) for writing test assertions.
o	Checking Status Code:
JavaScript
// In the "Tests" tab of a request
pm.test("Status code is 200 OK", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Status code is 201 Created", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(201);
});
o	Checking Response Body Content:
JavaScript
// Assuming a JSON response
var jsonData = pm.response.json();

pm.test("Response has a 'name' property", function () {
    pm.expect(jsonData).to.have.property("name");
});

pm.test("Patient name matches request", function () {
    // Assuming 'expectedName' was set as a variable or known
    // For example, if you sent "John Doe" in the request body
    pm.expect(jsonData.name).to.eql("John Doe");
});

pm.test("Patient ID is a non-empty string", function () {
    pm.expect(jsonData.id).to.be.a('string').and.to.not.be.empty;
});
o	Checking Response Headers:
JavaScript
pm.test("Content-Type header is application/json", function () {
    pm.expect(pm.response.headers.get('Content-Type')).to.include('application/json');
});
•	Chaining Requests (Capturing IDs into Environment Variables): This is essential for tests that depend on dynamically generated IDs.
o	Example: Capturing patient_id from POST /patient response: Place the following script in the "Tests" tab of the POST /patient request:
JavaScript
if (pm.response.code === 201) { // Check for successful creation
    try {
        var jsonData = pm.response.json();
        if (jsonData && jsonData.id) {
            pm.environment.set("patient_id", jsonData.id);
            console.log("Patient ID captured and set to environment variable: " + jsonData.id);
        } else {
            console.error("Could not find 'id' in the response JSON for POST /patient.");
        }
    } catch (e) {
        console.error("Failed to parse JSON response or access 'id': " + e);
    }
}
o	Similar scripts should be implemented for:
	Capturing experiment_id from the POST /experiment response and setting pm.environment.set("experiment_id", jsonData.id);.
	Capturing dataPointId from the POST /patient/{{patient_id}}/data response and setting pm.environment.set("data_point_id", jsonData.dataPointId); (assuming the ID is in jsonData.dataPointId).
Automated verification through scripts is far more reliable and efficient than manual inspection of every response field, especially for large and complex JSON structures. These scripts provide immediate feedback on whether a test has passed or failed based on predefined criteria. Furthermore, a Postman collection enhanced with such scripted tests and environment variables forms a solid foundation for full test automation. Using Newman, Postman's command-line Collection Runner, this entire test suite can be executed automatically, for instance, as part of a Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline, enabling rapid feedback on API changes.
